<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Galați</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Galați is a city and port on the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a>, in the historical region of Moldavia. As Moldavia's only port on the river, it grew in the 19th century as a grain-exporting free port and became the seat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-commission">European Commission</a> of the Danube, created in 1856. In the 20th century it became Romania's main centre of steelmaking and shipbuilding.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/galati.jpg" alt="Galați" width="320" height="187" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nica George este autorul imaginilor. Mi-a dat acordul lui pentru încărcarea lor pe Wikipedia. · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:DunareaSiPalatulNavigatiei.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Galați lies in south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a>, on the left bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> at the junction of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siret">Siret</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prut">Prut</a>, about 80 kilometres from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a>. It is the capital of Galați County in the historical region of Western Moldavia and one of the larger cities on the Danube. The nearest town is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/braila">Brăila</a>, 15 kilometres to the south, and the two have long been rivals. The city is built on three terraces, from the low Valley City area to the old town, now the centre, and the higher modern city.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The area was occupied in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> period, and the town developed from a Dacian settlement of the sixth and fifth centuries BCE beside a ford over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>. After the Dacian wars against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> in 101 to 102 and 105 to 106, the area became part of the Roman empire, and a strong fortress was built at Barboși to defend the ford. A document signed by Stephen II of Moldavia mentions Galați in 1445, and after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kiliya">Chilia</a> in 1484 it remained Moldavia's only port, for trade with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatars</a> plundered the town in 1710, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> opened a consulate in 1775, and Russian troops under General Mikhail Kamensky burned it during the Russo-Turkish War of 1787–92. After Russia annexed half of Moldavia in 1812, Galați was left as Moldavia's only port, and warships were built there. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> it was rebuilt on communist lines; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steel">steel</a> works began construction in 1960 and opened in 1966, when the population was 151,000.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Galați is an economic centre based on its port, its naval shipyard, the largest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a>, and the largest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steel">steel</a> works in the country, still commonly called Sidex. The University of Galați was founded in 1974. The Archdiocesan Cathedral of the Lower Danube was begun in 1906 and built over eleven years, and the Roman and Dacian site of Tirighina-Barboși has been excavated. The 2021 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> counted 217,851 inhabitants. In Bram Stoker's novel Dracula, Galați, spelled Galatz, is the port where the Count lands on his way back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transylvania">Transylvania</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Galați” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Wartime industry turned fortification guns into field and anti-aircraft artillery, and the Danube fleet had four river monitors built at <b>Galați</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-romania">Kingdom of Romania · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Grain exports through <b>Galați</b> and Brăila drove the economy.</blockquote><a href="/state/united-principalities">United Principalities</a></li></ul></section>
